    "result_text": "All 283 tests pass. The implementation is complete.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the `locality` field for owner creation. When an owner is created, the response now includes `locality` set to `\"local\"` if their city is the single most common city among existing owners at that moment, otherwise `\"remote\"`.\n\nChanges made, following the existing `Assign*` pipeline conventions:\n\n1. **`AssignLocality.java`** (new) \u2014 the OfficeFloor function. It tallies cities across existing owners (excluding the not-yet-saved owner), finds the single most common city, and sets `locality` to `\"local\"` only if exactly one city has the max count and it matches the owner's city; otherwise `\"remote\"`. Ties or an empty owner set \u2192 `\"remote\"`. Cities are grouped case-/whitespace-insensitively via the shared `DuplicateMatching.normalize`, consistent with `NormalizeCity`/`AssignSharesHousehold`.\n\n2. **`owners.POST.yml`** \u2014 wired `assignLocality` into the pipeline after `assignSharesHousehold` and before `save` (under the same `transaction` governance). It runs after `normalizeCity`, so the city is already canonicalized.\n\n3. **`Owner.java`** \u2014 added a persisted `locality` column with getter/setter (mirroring `sharesHousehold`). Persistence is required because the test reads it back via a subsequent `GET`.\n\n4. **Schema files** (hsqldb, h2, mysql, postgres) \u2014 added the `locality VARCHAR(20)` column.\n\n5. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 added the read-only `locality` property to the `Owner` schema, so MapStruct's `OwnerMapper` maps it into the generated `OwnerDto`.\n\nThe new `Cp18Tests` acceptance test and the full suite (283 tests) pass.",
